{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sole-curbable-stinkpot","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Impact, stated first\n\nThe scheduled **Reconcile Bridge** GitHub Actions workflow — the thing that keeps rebar and Jira in sync — **failed 12 times in 6 days** on two recurring ACLI (Atlassian CLI) faults. When it stalls, the bridge simply stops reconciling; the only reason anyone finds out is the **Reconciler Heartbeat Canary**, which fired 6 times on 2026-08-07 reporting the bridge had been dead for over six hours.\n\nThis was found by an automated ops error sweep of GitHub Actions over 2026-08-05 -> 2026-08-12T03:30Z. There was no open ticket for either mode.\n\n## Mode 1 — ACLI credential expiry (6 occurrences)\n\nFirst 2026-08-07T12:48Z, most recent 2026-08-11T10:16Z.\n\n    ACLI stderr: ✗ Error: unauthorized: use `acli [product] auth login` to authenticate\n    ERROR: reconcile_once raised: Command `[acli, jira, workitem, search, ...]` returned non-zero exit status 1\n    Reconcile failed (exit 1)\n\nThe credential the workflow authenticates with expires, and nothing renews it or reports the expiry as anything other than a generic red run. Because it recurs on a cadence rather than once, this is a **credential-lifecycle defect, not a one-off outage**.\n\n## Mode 2 — ACLI aborts mid-pass (6 occurrences)\n\nFirst 2026-08-06T22:58Z, most recent 2026-08-11T21:02Z.\n\n    ACLI stderr: ✗ Error: command cancelled\n\nThis one always appears **after a long batch of inbound link-apply work**, i.e. late in a pass rather than at startup. The pass then dies with no clean exit, so a partially-applied reconcile is left behind. The leading hypothesis is an ACLI-side timeout or rate-limit under a long pass, but that is **not yet confirmed** — the log gives only the bare `command cancelled` string with no status code.\n\n## Why the two are filed together\n\nThey share a subsystem, a workflow, and a blast radius, and they interleave in the same week; separating them would split one operational story. If investigation shows Mode 2 is a downstream effect of an expiring session (a credential dying *during* a long pass would plausibly surface as a cancel), they are literally the same bug. That relationship should be checked first.\n\n## Evidence trail\n\n- Heartbeat canary true-positives caused by these stalls, 2026-08-07T01:23Z -> 05:29Z (6 runs): `##[error]Reconciler is stale — Last successful run was 6h 32m ago (threshold: 2h).`\n- The canary firing correctly is the good news here: detection works. The gap is that **nothing prevents or auto-remediates the ACLI faults themselves**.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The ACLI credential used by the scheduled Reconcile Bridge either does not expire on this cadence, or its expiry is detected and renewed automatically rather than surfacing as a failed run.\n- [ ] An ACLI auth failure is reported distinguishably from other reconcile failures, so an operator can tell a credential problem from a data problem without opening the log.\n- [ ] The relationship between Mode 1 and Mode 2 is determined and recorded: either Mode 2 is shown to be a distinct fault with its own cause, or it is shown to be the expiry surfacing mid-pass.\n- [ ] A pass aborted by Mode 2 cannot leave a partially-applied reconcile with no record; the abort is either prevented or made recoverable on the next pass.\n\n## Notes\n\nFiled by an automated ops error sweep. No code was changed and no file_impact is recorded — the fault is in the ACLI credential/session lifecycle used by the scheduled workflow, and naming source files confidently would require investigation this sweep deliberately did not do.","id":"9a71-ba9b-89d0-4cb8","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Scheduled Reconcile Bridge fails on ACLI auth expiry and mid-pass 'command cancelled', silently stalling the Jira bridge for hours"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786505756081994001,"uuid":"6acbcef2-ce4d-4981-8b5d-c1675e869572"}